WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.420
- My next guest is a former
monk turned bestselling author.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.800
That doesn't happen every day.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.320
His podcast "On Purpose with Jay Shetty"

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.530
is the number one health
podcast on the planet.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.000
He's interviewed everyone

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.950
from President Biden to Oprah Winfrey.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.600
He's even interviewed the
man sitting next to me

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.700
that can sing, lies and deception.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.424
Please welcome Jay Shetty, everybody.

00:00:20.424 --> 00:00:24.271
(crowd cheers and applauds)

00:00:24.271 --> 00:00:27.354
(bright jazzy music)

00:00:28.616 --> 00:00:32.366
(crowd continues applauding)

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.260
You, I have to lead with this,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.880
you have the most beautiful eyes ever.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:45.860
They are like-

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.460
- Oh, thank you.
- so beautiful.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.960
- Well I can't take any
credit for them, so-

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.750
- Okay, well done god.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.583
Have y'all met before?

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:54.083
You know each other, right?

00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:54.916
- Yes, yes.
- Obviously?

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:55.749
Okay, okay.

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:56.582
- Yeah, I've had the fortune of-

00:00:56.582 --> 00:00:57.415
- We just met in person.

00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:58.248
- We did, just earlier

00:00:58.248 --> 00:00:59.081
today.
- Just earlier today.

00:00:59.081 --> 00:00:59.914
- Oh!
- But I've had Dwayne

00:00:59.914 --> 00:01:01.980
on the show, and we actually
did it virtually through Zoom.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.120
- Yeah.
- And, the connection

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.260
was bad and all this kind of stuff.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.220
- Terrible.
- But his spirit

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.260
still came through, so it was beautiful.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.690
You were phenomenal.
- Yeah, it was great.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:09.523
- Thank you so much.
- Thank you.

00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:10.790
- Have you always been
good at public speaking?

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.750
- Definitely not.
- Okay.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.220
I'm, I get nervous speaking.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.053
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:15.053 --> 00:01:15.886
I need to tell you a story

00:01:15.886 --> 00:01:18.000
about my first ever time public speaking.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.529
I was seven years old.

00:01:19.529 --> 00:01:22.410
There was an assembly at
school, a cultural assembly,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.320
and my mother loved to see me on stage.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.896
So she dressed me up in a
traditional Indian outfit,

00:01:27.896 --> 00:01:29.800
parts of me were falling out.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.100
And then she'd asked me to sing,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.140
I have a horrific singing voice.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.870
She'd asked me to sing
something in my native language

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.536
on stage in front of my school.

00:01:38.536 --> 00:01:40.595
So I get up there, I start singing,

00:01:40.595 --> 00:01:42.195
and talking and singing.

00:01:42.195 --> 00:01:45.656
And everyone bursts
out laughing, everyone.

00:01:45.656 --> 00:01:46.560
- Ugh.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.660
- I'm glad you didn't
start laughing right now.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.970
And then, I forgot the words

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.520
so I looked down to see
if I could read the words,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.237
and I couldn't because my
tears had smudged the words.

00:01:56.237 --> 00:01:58.290
(crowd gasps)
And so then I forgot the words

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.880
and everyone started laughing more.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.555
- This is the worst story ever.

00:02:01.555 --> 00:02:02.610
(crowd laughs)
- Yeah.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.170
And then, to make it worse, my teacher

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.420
came on stage and put her arm around me.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.493
- Oh no.
- And walked me off stage.

00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:10.032
(crowd awing)

00:02:10.032 --> 00:02:10.865
And that was my first experience

00:02:10.865 --> 00:02:12.660
of public speaking, so...
(crowd laughing)

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.280
- And you're, here you are now.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.113
This is incredible.

00:02:15.113 --> 00:02:16.411
- Yes, you can do anything.

00:02:16.411 --> 00:02:17.610
- I know, I'm just saying like the fact

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.500
that that didn't deter
you, and you were like

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.240
I've got this!
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.140
Yeah, it was painful.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.970
- I don't know if I'd ever
speak in public again.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.470
- I know. That's what it felt like.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.875
- Well Jay spent three
years as a Hindu monk,

00:02:27.875 --> 00:02:29.910
and later wrote a bestselling book,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:30.870
called "Think Like a Monk."

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.808
So you've talked a lot
about why you became a monk,

00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:34.856
but not why you maybe got out.

00:02:34.856 --> 00:02:36.570
So why? What was the reason?

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.390
- Well, I was learning just
so many incredible insights

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.220
about mindfulness and meditation.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.480
And I really had this hunger

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.310
and desire to share it with the world.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.710
But at the time, now it makes sense.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.510
But back then, I was actually quite scared

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.620
when I came back to London.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.461
And I was stuck about where to start.

00:02:51.461 --> 00:02:53.490
And I remember talking
to one of my mentors

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.310
and saying, I have so many
ideas about how to help people,

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.050
but I don't know how to get started.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.355
And I'm guessing some of you
have felt that way before.

00:03:00.355 --> 00:03:02.400
And he gave me some brilliant advice

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.330
I'd love to share with you.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.163
- Yeah.

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:07.050
- He said to me that Jay, you should try

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.560
and open every door you possibly can.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.990
Some of the doors will close on you

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.320
and you should let them close.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.680
But the doors that remain open,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.754
those are the ones that are meant for you

00:03:18.754 --> 00:03:20.735
and you should keep walking through them.

00:03:20.735 --> 00:03:22.260
And I feel like I'm sitting here today

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.510
because I've just continued
to walk through the doors

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.160
that stayed open for me.
- Yeah.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.941
Rather the ones that shut on my face.

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:28.799
(crowd clapping)
- I mean, you can use that

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:30.439
in so many different ways.
- Absolutely.

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:32.398
- Like relationships, like everyth.

00:03:32.398 --> 00:03:34.140
You can use that, it's a great metaphor.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.300
And it's that time of year when some of us

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.390
have started off with
really good intentions,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.830
and plans to make pivots in our lives.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:44.760
Not me, I'm conquering them all.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.950
But some of you may have
lost a little steam.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.410
We have several people in our audience

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.510
with questions about
their life pivots for Jay,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.399
so let's answer some of those right now.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.890
Bailey has a question
about moving forward.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.110
So Bailey, go ahead.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:59.220
- Hi, Jay.
- Hey Bailey.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.380
- My name is Bailey, and I was diagnosed

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.968
with brain cancer and the age of 24.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:06.810
So I had about a year and a
half going through treatment.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:07.890
And during that time, I listened

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.510
to your podcast all the time.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.448
I listened before, but I extra
listened during all of that.

00:04:12.448 --> 00:04:14.430
And so, you know, that was a really,

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.107
my life was put on pause for a little bit.

00:04:16.107 --> 00:04:18.240
And then, I finished my treatment.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.091
I'm now 26.
- Wow.

00:04:20.091 --> 00:04:24.273
(audience applauding)
- Thank you.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.980
Yeah, so now I'm 26 and I'm
going back to the real world,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.230
kind of, my new real world, I should say.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.331
I'm in grad school.

00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:35.400
And, I had like some
different questions to ask you

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.860
because kind of like
my life was put on hold

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.960
for a little bit, while
everyone else my age

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.490
in their twenties was moving forward,

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:42.900
I was kind of on pause.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.460
So I'm wondering if you had any advice

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.395
when you go through like
a setback in your life,

00:04:46.395 --> 00:04:48.030
how to move forward from it?

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.940
And how to, what's the
best advice to you got?

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.950
- Oh my God, that's a huge question.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.090
It's a good one.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.266
- Bailey, wow, first of
all, you're so inspiring.

00:04:57.266 --> 00:04:59.334
Like listening to you is inspiring,

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:00.167
(crowd clapping)
honestly.

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.290
That's unbelievable, it's unbelievable.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.380
I was about to run up the steps and try

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.029
and give you a hug, but I
don't think it's possible.

00:05:06.029 --> 00:05:07.050
But, maybe later.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.050
But honestly, listening to your
story makes me realize that

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.009
what it sounds like you're
dealing with is grief.

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:18.272
And a lot of us think of grief
as loss of someone we love,

00:05:18.272 --> 00:05:21.826
but grief can also be the
loss of a life we once had,

00:05:21.826 --> 00:05:25.211
or a time we thought we should
have had or could have had.

00:05:25.211 --> 00:05:28.050
And I was speaking to
Katie Couric yesterday,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.090
and she shared this really
beautiful thing with me

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.710
that I'd love to share with you.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.531
She was saying to me that grief is like

00:05:33.531 --> 00:05:36.991
walking around with a rock in your pocket.

00:05:36.991 --> 00:05:39.254
And she was saying that in the beginning,

00:05:39.254 --> 00:05:41.820
every time you move, you feel that rock.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.530
It might even scratch you, it hurts you.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.920
And then she said, as time goes on,

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.510
the weight of that rock

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.820
starts to feel less and less and less.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.192
It's not that it goes away.

00:05:52.192 --> 00:05:55.830
And, it goes less and less and less

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.990
because you are growing at the same time.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.910
And I think one of the
best ways you could grow,

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.312
and I'd love for you to try this,

00:06:01.312 --> 00:06:04.170
is keep sharing your
inspiring stories with others.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.534
I think if you're helping other
people carry their weight,

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:08.411
then your weight starts to feel less.

00:06:08.411 --> 00:06:09.990
So I hope you'll try that out.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.020
And keep inspiring Bailey,
your story's phenomenal,

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.066
genuinely.
(crowd clapping)

00:06:14.066 --> 00:06:15.300
- Thank you so much, I appreciate it.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.635
- Thank you so much, big hugs.

00:06:16.635 --> 00:06:17.468
Big air hugs.

00:06:17.468 --> 00:06:18.301
(crowd continues clapping)

00:06:18.301 --> 00:06:19.584
- I do think it's nice that you're saying

00:06:19.584 --> 00:06:20.430
to shift your focus on others,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.784
if you're going through a
hard time, it helps a bit.

00:06:22.784 --> 00:06:23.820
- Absolutely.
- Not the escapism of it,

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.151
but just to lessen its power.

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:28.410
- Yes, yes yes.
- You know? Yeah.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:29.730
Thank you so much Bailey,

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.000
and congratulations, man.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.858
(audience applauding)

00:06:32.858 --> 00:06:36.393
That's a huge thing, that
happens in your life.

00:06:36.393 --> 00:06:39.660
Well Headier has a question
about navigating rejection,

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.220
which I think we all relate to.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.560
- Hi Jay, I'm Headier.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.560
So a little bit about
me, I'm 25 years old.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.750
I just graduated pharmacy
school two years ago.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.110
And in that time, I still have
yet to find a permanent job.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.110
My mom and my dad tell me
to put myself out there,

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.780
to ask friends, to apply
at different locations.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.356
But unfortunately, I just feel stuck.

00:07:00.356 --> 00:07:02.859
I feel tired, I feel left behind.

00:07:02.859 --> 00:07:05.670
And I don't, I have a
fear of asking for help.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:06.990
I have the fear of rejection

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.000
from like the hospitals and other places.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.160
So I just don't know what to do.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.420
- Actually, I wanna role play a little bit

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.410
right now, if you're okay

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:14.648
with it.
- Yeah, of course.

00:07:14.648 --> 00:07:15.614
- So can we get a little ringtone sound?

00:07:15.614 --> 00:07:16.920
Yes?
♪ Ring ring ♪

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:17.910
- There you go, perfect.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.410
- Hey Headier, it's Jay. How's it going?

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.460
- Hey Jay, how you doing?

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:21.870
- I'm doing really well,
I'm doing really well.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:22.703
What's up?

00:07:22.703 --> 00:07:23.580
- Nothing much.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:24.660
- Well, I heard that you graduated

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:25.493
a couple of years ago.

00:07:25.493 --> 00:07:26.326
How's it all going?

00:07:26.326 --> 00:07:28.773
- Oh you know it's
going, just living life.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.024
- Is there anything I could do for you?

00:07:32.024 --> 00:07:34.580
- I don't know, I don't know what to...

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:35.413
(audience laughs)
I truly,

00:07:35.413 --> 00:07:38.640
I guess maybe help me contact,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.460
if you have connections
that can help me, I guess.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.130
- Yeah. And what kind of
connections would you need?

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.510
- In hospitals.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.880
That's where I'm mainly
looking at right now.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:48.713
- That's brilliant Headier.

00:07:48.713 --> 00:07:50.580
I'm gonna reach out to
my network, for real

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.020
and see what I can do for you.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.019
So thank you so much
Headier for asking for help.

00:07:54.019 --> 00:07:54.852
- Thank you, thank you.
- I appreciate it.

00:07:54.852 --> 00:07:55.685
Well done.

00:07:55.685 --> 00:07:57.260
Thank you. It wasn't so hard, right?

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:00.736
(crowd clapping)
It wasn't so hard?

00:08:00.736 --> 00:08:03.294
- It's so funny because
I love how you started

00:08:03.294 --> 00:08:05.406
because I, that is the thing,

00:08:05.406 --> 00:08:07.650
I think a lot of us have a hard time with.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.350
Like, I'm always there,
like I love helping people,

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:14.190
but it is very hard, I had
to learn how to be able to...

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.498
'Cause I think sometimes we think

00:08:15.498 --> 00:08:17.970
it's a sign of weakness or something.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.080
- Yes.
- And it's not.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.411
- We think we're taking
something from someone.

00:08:21.411 --> 00:08:23.811
But actually, you're
giving them an opportunity

00:08:23.811 --> 00:08:26.880
to shed kindness, to spread kindness.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:27.930
- Mm.
- And anyone

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.430
who wants to do that is gonna take it

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.210
as an opportunity to want to support you.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.310
- Yeah.
- And so,

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.270
I want you to try that out.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.816
Just practice it a few more
times and you'll nail it.

00:08:35.816 --> 00:08:37.290
- Thank you so much Headier,

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:38.478
just for sharing.
(crowd clapping)

00:08:38.478 --> 00:08:40.837
- Yeah, thanks Headier.
- And good luck.

00:08:40.837 --> 00:08:42.979
Maybe someone listening right now

00:08:42.979 --> 00:08:44.730
on TV heard that.
- I know.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:46.740
- She would make a great
employee at a hospital,

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:47.940
I'm just saying.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.380
Well On Purpose with Jay Shetty

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.240
is available wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.459
Jay is also the Chief Purpose Officer

00:08:53.459 --> 00:08:55.800
for Calm, an app where you can find

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.100
his daily mindfulness
series, so check it out.

